#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186082924 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "345d6df7-2ada-5c2e-8146-7c779afae3af" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Center of a Vertical Hyperbola Given an Equation in Expanded Form"}] :question/id #uuid "e930a29a-6adf-5aaa-9b44-86dd8d570ae7" :question/math-academy-id "e-5354" :question/problem "What is the center of the hyperbola\n$4 y^2 - 10 x^2 - 8 y - 40 x = 76$?" :question/worked-solution "The equation of a vertical hyperbola centered at $(h,k)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{(y-k)^2}{a^2} - \\dfrac{(x-h)^2}{b^2}=1\n$$\n\nTo rewrite the equation of the hyperbola in the above form, we need to group $x$ and $y$ terms and complete the squares as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n4y^{2} - 10x^{2} - 8y - 40x &= 76 \\\\\n4y^{2} - 8y - 10x^{2} - 40x &= 76 \\\\\n4[y^{2} - 2y] - 10[x^{2} + 4x] &= 76 \\\\\n4[(y - 1)^{2} - 1] - 10[(x + 2)^{2} - 4] &= 76 \\\\\n4(y - 1)^{2} - 4 - 10(x + 2)^{2} + 40 &= 76 \\\\\n4(y - 1)^{2} - 10(x + 2)^{2} &= 76 - 40 + 4 \\\\\n4(y - 1)^{2} - 10(x + 2)^{2} &= 40 \\\\\n((y - 1)^{2})/(10) - ((x + 2)^{2})/(4) &= 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the center is $(-2,1)$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186082991 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f99d4fec-bc58-5a09-9fac-38b46f233489" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Center of a Horizontal Hyperbola"}] :question/id #uuid "0caa1041-a564-5428-9838-20cea32fdf17" :question/math-academy-id "e-6775" :question/problem "What is the center of the hyperbola $\\dfrac{(x-1)^2}{9} - \\dfrac{(y-2)^2}{4} = 1$?" :question/worked-solution "The equation of a horizontal hyperbola centered at $(h,k)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{(x-h)^2}{a^2} - \\dfrac{(y-k)^2}{b^2}=1\n$$\n\nIn this case, we have $h = 1$ and $k = 2$.\n\nTherefore, the center of the hyperbola is $(1,2)$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186082992 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "cdab0134-e416-51cf-89fa-73ad4a2c2eb9" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Center of a Vertical Hyperbola"}] :question/id #uuid "8e31f5b5-ff87-5c45-b143-66dfdcb4ab8c" :question/math-academy-id "e-6776" :question/problem "What is the center of the hyperbola $\\dfrac{(y+3)^2}{16} - \\dfrac{(x-1)^2}{9} = 1$?" :question/worked-solution "The equation of a vertical hyperbola centered at $(h,k)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{(y-k)^2}{a^2} - \\dfrac{(x-h)^2}{b^2}=1\n$$\n\nNote that the given equation can be rewritten as follows:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{(y-(-3))^2}{16} - \\dfrac{(x-1)^2}{9}=1\n$$\n\nIn this case, we have $h = 1$ and $k=-3$.\n\nTherefore, the center of the hyperbola is $(1,-3)$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186082993 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "223e29c9-1bb5-51ee-9ec8-4d5c15363951" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Center of a Horizontal Hyperbola Given an Equation in Expanded Form"}] :question/id #uuid "af591507-e248-53d2-a391-e053cdc3fdea" :question/math-academy-id "e-6777" :question/problem "What is the center of the hyperbola\n$x^2 - 4 y^2- 2 x + 16 y = 31$?" :question/worked-solution "The equation of a horizontal hyperbola centered at $(h,k)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{(x-h)^2}{a^2} - \\dfrac{(y-k)^2}{b^2}=1\n$$\n\nTo rewrite the equation of the hyperbola in the above form, we need to group $x$ and $y$ terms and complete the squares, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx^{2} - 4y^{2} - 2x + 16y &= 31 \\\\\nx^{2} - 2x - 4y^{2} + 16y &= 31 \\\\\nx^{2} - 2x - 4[y^{2} - 4y] &= 31 \\\\\n(x - 1)^{2} - 1 - 4[(y - 2)^{2} - 4] &= 31 \\\\\n(x - 1)^{2} - 1 - 4(y - 2)^{2} + 16 &= 31 \\\\\n(x - 1)^{2} - 4(y - 2)^{2} &= 31 + 1 - 16 \\\\\n(x - 1)^{2} - 4(y - 2)^{2} &= 16 \\\\\n((x - 1)^{2})/(16) - ((y - 2)^{2})/(4) &= 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the center is $(1,2)$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186263984 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f99d4fec-bc58-5a09-9fac-38b46f233489" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Center of a Horizontal Hyperbola"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "abcfb624-f2a3-5d93-85c8-81747f7f5169" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(5,6)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263986} {:answer/id #uuid "6c6bd005-39cb-5b95-8ed0-482ecffda449" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2,-4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263987} {:answer/id #uuid "8f50a3e4-875c-5112-bbd3-4af4b3b40f3d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(6,5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263988} {:answer/id #uuid "03792036-0cf0-5d15-ab62-73a460fddbaa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(4,-2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263989} {:answer/id #uuid "4648ecb8-4b69-5698-b0d5-a39dcb693fbc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-2,4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263990}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4648ecb8-4b69-5698-b0d5-a39dcb693fbc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-2,4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263990} :answer-field/id #uuid "d8a525c2-17a1-5b6c-a397-8097118fff3b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263985}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a3668991-b7c7-5625-859d-527dc7e0482e" :question/math-academy-id "q-116082" :question/problem "What is the center of the hyperbola $\\frac{(x+2)^{2}}{5}-\\frac{(y-4)^{2}}{6}=1?$" :question/worked-solution "The equation of a horizontal hyperbola centered at $(h,k)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{(x-h)^{2}}{{a}^{2}}-\\frac{(y-k)^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\nNote that the given equation can be rewritten as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{(x-(-2))^{2}}{(\\sqrt{5})^{2}}-\\frac{(y-4)^{2}}{(\\sqrt{6})^{2}}=1\n$$\n\nIn this case, $h=-2$ and $k=4.$\n\nTherefore, the center of the hyperbola is $(-2,4).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186264029 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "cdab0134-e416-51cf-89fa-73ad4a2c2eb9" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the 